                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1335-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_sc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Supercardioid" title="Supercardioid" ></span>Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 865</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Supercardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Sennheiser e 865 is a handheld supercardioid condenser mic for stage use. This is the only condenser vocal mic in the evolution line.</p><p>The e865 was nominated for a TEC award in 2001.</p><p>The mic uses uses an electret condenser capsule with a response of 40 - 20kHz. The mic is rated at 150 dB SPL.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/sennheiser/e865-sting.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/sennheiser/e865-sting-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="e 865, Sting Signature Edition" width="200" /></a>Three models of the mic are available:</p><ul><li><b>e865</b> - microphone, MZQ 800 clamp, storage pouch</li><li><b>e865 S</b> - as above, with a lockable, noiseless on/off switch.</li><li><b>Sting Signature Edition</b> - e865 with Sting&#8217;s signature, presumably packaged as the original e865 with clip and pouch</li></ul><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sennheiser/e-865">Sennheiser Electronics Corporation e 865</a> is also known as: e865s, evolution 865, e865.</p>
